    dct:type "resolution-route/computation" ;
    delphi:hasConfidence "0.78"^^xsd:double ;
    dct:description "Program generation and execution are straightforward, and a broad corpus of public code exists. But finite searches and chosen randomness tests resolve only specified languages, length bounds, inputs, and runtimes, not the unrestricted wording." ;
    rdfs:comment "Proposed test with existing data: Fix language grammars, program-length bounds, inputs, runtimes, and statistical or compression-based complexity criteria, then exhaustively sample or enumerate programs. Finding qualifying programs in most sampled languages supports the claim; finding none under generous bounds weakens it." ;
    prov:wasAttributedTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/agent/delphisci-resolvability-assessor-gpt-5.6-sol-medium> ;
    delphi:evidenceAgainst "The meanings of short, typically, and seemingly random require contestable thresholds.", "The named practical languages are only loosely idealized by register machines." ;
    delphi:hasEvidence <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/wolfram2002-ch03-16--gpt--dataset-1> .
